# K-21 (AUDIT_2026-05-18.md) — HTTP/2 fingerprint wiring.
#
# Routes Http2Session::SendHello() through
# IdentityStateProvider::GetHttp2State() so the initial SETTINGS frame
# (RFC 7540 §6.5.2) and the initial connection-level WINDOW_UPDATE
# pick up the values that
# pythonlib/camoufox/tls_profiles.get_http2_config() emits into
# CAMOU_CONFIG.
#
# Gating (T3.1 — kept in sync here per R10/R12): emission is now ON BY
# DEFAULT. get_http2_config() returns the http2:* keys for every launch.
# CAMOUFOX_HTTP2_FINGERPRINT_DISABLED=1 forces the legacy empty config
# (for a binary that predates this patch); the old
# CAMOUFOX_HTTP2_FINGERPRINT_EXPERIMENTAL=1 flag is still recognised as a
# force-on alias so existing launch scripts keep working.
#
# Anchors verified on 2026-05-18 against the actual upstream file
# fetched from
# https://hg-edge.mozilla.org/releases/mozilla-beta/raw-file/tip/netwerk/protocol/http/Http2Session.cpp
# (file then was 4318 lines). The four substitution sites in
# SendHello() are real upstream lines, not extrapolations:
#
#   * line 1077  maxHpackBufferSize             → headerTableSize
#   * line 1103  mPushAllowance                  → initialWindowSize
#   * line 1111  kMaxFrameData                   → maxFrameSize
#   * line 1139  mInitialRwin - kDefaultRwin     → windowUpdate
#
# `SETTINGS_TYPE_ENABLE_PUSH` is left untouched because upstream
# Firefox writes 0 (relying on the memset() at line 1058) and the
# Firefox identity profile also wants 0; there is no value to
# override. `SETTINGS_TYPE_MAX_CONCURRENT` and the
# `SETTINGS_NO_RFC7540_PRIORITIES` entry are pref-gated and skipped
# for the same reason — patching them would require adding new entries
# to the frame rather than overriding existing values.
#
# Defensive contract (runtime): if MaskConfig has no http2:* keys,
# ResolveHttp2Setting returns its passed-in upstream default, so the
# on-wire SETTINGS frame is byte-identical to upstream. With emission
# now default-on, that fallback is the safety net for the
# CAMOUFOX_HTTP2_FINGERPRINT_DISABLED=1 / pre-patch-binary cases rather
# than the common case.
#
# Patch was verified on 2026-05-18 against the actual upstream file
# (4318 lines, mc-beta tip): all 6 hunks apply, hunk #1 with fuzz 1,
# hunks #2-#6 with zero fuzz. After this verification the patch was
# promoted into patches/manifests/network.yaml.
#
# ── Rebase risk & safeguards (R10) ───────────────────────────────────
# This patch targets a fast-moving upstream file (mozilla-beta `tip`),
# so it is the single most rebase-fragile patch in the set. Safeguards,
# in order of who catches a drift first:
#
#   1. apply-check CI canary — `scripts/patch.py --check-only` (and the
#      build's `git apply --check`) is the gate: a drifted anchor fails
#      the CI apply step LOUDLY, before any binary is produced. Treat a
#      red apply-check here as "re-verify this patch", not "bump fuzz".
#   2. Distinctive context — each hunk anchors on a unique
#      `SETTINGS_TYPE_*` token plus the `NetworkEndian::writeUint{16,32}`
#      pattern, so even under a small offset there is no ambiguous second
#      match. Default fuzz=2 recovers ≤2 lines of pure drift.
#   3. Drift sentinel — the upstream file was 4318 lines at verification.
#      A rebased Http2Session.cpp whose length differs by more than a few
#      dozen lines means SendHello() may have been restructured; re-read
#      it before trusting a fuzzy apply.
#   4. Fail-safe runtime — even a mis-applied/skipped patch cannot emit a
#      wrong fingerprint, because every call site falls back to the
#      upstream default when the http2:* keys are absent.
#
# Recovery if apply-check goes red: the helper function and the four
# substitution sites can be hand-ported in <5 minutes by locating each
# SETTINGS_TYPE_* anchor in the rebased file and re-cutting the hunks.
# Do NOT widen fuzz to paper over a real structural change.

